Actually, for the main deliverable (existence over a pinned operator class) the key structure is:
```
structure FiniteHeatOperator (X) [Fintype X] [DecidableEq X] where
  L : Matrix X X ℝ
  symmetric : Lᵀ = L
  conservative : ∀ x, ∑ y, L x y = 0
  offdiag_pos : ∀ x y, x ≠ y → 0 < L x y
```
This is our hypothesis class — it PINS the operator (up to the matrix), and existence of the heat kernel with the PDE is provable for each such operator. This is the honest "hypothesis class that pins the operator" in the finite setting.

Then the "corrected-domain existence statement with pinned operator" can be stated as:
```
theorem finite_pinned_existence :
    ∀ (L : FiniteHeatOperator X), ∃ K : X → X → ℝ → ℝ, 
      (∀ x y t, 0 < t → 0 < K x y t) ∧
      (∀ x y t, 0 < t → HasDerivAt (fun s => K x y s) (L.laplacian (fun z => K z y t) x) t) ∧
      (∀ y t, 0 < t → ∫ x, K x y t ∂count = 1) ∧
      (∀ f : X → ℝ, ∀ y, Tendsto (fun t => ∫ x, K x y t * f x ∂count) (𝓝[>] 0) (𝓝 (f y)))
